MULTI-TASKING(3)            MULTI-TASKING FUNCTIONS            MULTI-TASKING(3)NAME       multi-tasking - functions for concurrent programmingSYNOPSIS       #include multi_tasking.f83       multi-tasking ( -- )       TASK ( -- )       +queue ( task -- queue) private       +status ( task -- status) private       +sp ( task -- sp) private       +s0 ( task -- s0) private       +ip ( task -- ip) private       +rp ( task -- rp) private       +r0 ( task -- r0) private       +fp ( task -- fp) private       +ep ( task -- ep) private       TASK-STATUS-CODES ( -- )       TERMINATED ( -- 0)       READY ( -- 1)       RUNNING ( -- 2)       IOWAITING ( -- 3)       WAITING ( -- 4)       DELAYED ( -- 5)       foreground ( -- task)       running ( -- task)       user ( offset -- )       task ( users parameters returns code -- task)       resume ( task -- )       schedule ( task -- )       detach ( -- )       terminate ( -- )       >terminate ( -- addr)       deactivate ( queue task -- )        activate ( task -- )        delay ( n -- )       join ( task -- )        who ( -- )        SEMAPHORE ( n -- )       +waiting ( semaphore -- waiting) private       +count ( semaphore -- count) private       mutex ( -- )        signal ( semaphore -- )        ?wait ( semaphore -- flag)        wait ( semaphore -- )               COMMUNICATION-MODELS ( -- )       ONE-TO-ONE ( -- 0)       ONE-TO-MANY ( -- 1)       MANY-TO-ONE ( -- 2)       CHAN ( mode -- )       +sent ( chan -- sent) private       +received ( chan -- received) private       +data ( chan -- data) private       ?avail ( chan -- flag)       send ( data chan -- )        receive ( chan -- data)         RENDEZVOUS ( -- )       +arg ( rendezvous -- arg) private       +res ( rendezvous -- res) private       accept ( -- arg) immediate       accept.end ( res -- ) immediate       ?awaiting ( -- boolean) immediate       task.type ( parameters returns -- )       +users ( task.type -- user) private       +parameters ( task.type -- parameters) private       +returns ( task.type -- returns) private       +body ( task.type -- body) private       bytes ( size -- )         struct ( -- )       byte ( -- )       word ( -- )       long ( -- )       ptr ( -- )       enum ( -- )       task.body ( -- )        task.end ( -- )       field ( size -- ) private       make ( task.type -- task)       new ( -- task)DESCRIPTION       Kernel and forth level extension to allow concurrent programming       with tasks, semaphores, channels and rendezvous.multi-tasking ( -- )       Vocabulary containing the multi-tasking extensions to the Forth 83       Standard. These extensions are realized both as primitives and on       the forth level.TASK ( -- )       Definition of the instance structure of a task. Created by        "task".+queue ( task -- queue) private       Field access of system queue of task instances.+status ( task -- status) private       Field access of task status. See "TASK-STATUS-CODES" for       possible codes. Stored as a enumerate value, enum.+sp ( task -- sp) private       Field access of task parameter stack pointer. Stored as a       pointer, ptr.+s0 ( task -- s0) private       Field access of task bottom of parameter stack pointer.       Stored as a pointer, ptr.+ip ( task -- ip) private       Field access of task instruction pointer. Stored as a       pointer, ptr.+rp ( task -- rp) private       Field access of task return stack pointer. Stored as a       pointer, ptr.+r0 ( task -- r0) private       Field access of task return stack bottom pointer. Stored as a       pointer, ptr.+fp ( task -- fp) private       Field access of task argument frame pointer. Stored as a       pointer, ptr.+ep ( task -- ep) private       Field access of task exception frame pointer. Stored as        a pointer, ptr.TASK-STATUS-CODES ( -- )       Enumerate defining the possible task status codes.TERMINATED ( -- 0)       Task status code for terminated tasks.READY ( -- 1)       Task status code for newly created tasks, ready for scheduling.RUNNING ( -- 2)       Task status code for running, scheduled, tasks.IOWAITING ( -- 3)       Task status code for task waiting for IO. The foreground task       is currently the only task allowed to perform io-operations,       as these are not reentrant.WAITING ( -- 4)       Task status code for task performing a generic wait operation.DELAYED ( -- 5)       Task status code for task performing "delay". Will resume        RUNNING after the "delay" operation.foreground ( -- task)       Variable containing a pointer to the foreground task.running ( -- task)       Variable containing a pointer to the current running task.user ( offset -- )       Used in the following form to create a local, user, variable       within the task instance:               <offset> user <user-name>       The offset is a relative address from the task pointer.task ( users parameters returns code -- task)       Used to create a task instance. All parameters, except code,       are sizes in bytes. Code is a pointer to forth-level code.       Returns a pointer to a task.resume ( task -- )       Activates the given task. The task must be a member of the       runnable tasks, i.e., scheduled and waiting for activation.schedule ( task -- )       Schedules the given task and activates it immediately. detach ( -- )       Deactivate the current running task and resumes the next       task in the queue over runnable tasks.terminate ( -- )       Terminates the current running task and resumes the next task       in the runnable task queue.>terminate ( -- addr)       Constant containing a pointer to the terminate function. Used       by "task" to automatically terminate task which arrive at the       end of their task body, i.e., forth-level code section.deactivate ( queue task -- )        Removes a task from the queue of runnable task and inserts       it into the given queue. The next runnable task is resumed.activate ( task -- )        Inserts a task first in the queue of runnable task and resumed.        The task is assumed to have be scheduled and deactivated. delay ( n -- )       Delays a task a number of task switches. The delay is not       real-time but relative time. The task status is "DELAYED"       during the delay.join ( task -- )        Delay a task until the task given as parameter terminates.       The task status is "WAITING" during the join delay.       who ( -- )        Displays the current state of the queue of runnable tasks.SEMAPHORE ( n -- )       Used as follows:               <initial-value> SEMAPHORE <semaphore-name>       to create and initiate a named semaphore.+waiting ( semaphore -- count) private       Field access to semaphore queue for waiting tasks.+count ( semaphore -- count) private       Field access to semaphore value.mutex ( -- )        Used as follows:               mutex <semaphore-name>       to create a semaphore for mutual exclusion. Should not be       used within other structures as this definition is not a       structure definition but a code definition.signal ( semaphore -- )        If there is a waiting task on the semaphore this task is       resumed else the semaphore counter is incremented.?wait ( semaphore -- flag)        Checks the semaphore counter to determine if a "wait"       operation will make a task wait. Returns true in this case.wait ( semaphore -- )        Dequeues the task and places it into the semaphore waiting       queue if the semaphore counter is zero otherwise the counter       is decrements and the task continues. The task status is        "WAITING" during the semaphore wait delay.       COMMUNICATION-MODELS ( -- )       Enumerate type used to select the operation type of a channel.ONE-TO-ONE ( -- 0)       The channel is to be operated in a one-to-one manner.ONE-TO-MANY ( -- 1)       The channel is to be operated in a one-to-many manner. Several       receiving tasks are possible on the channel. Data sent on the        channel is maintained from corruption.MANY-TO-ONE ( -- 2)       Selects the many-to-one communication mode of a channel. Several       sending tasks are allowed to communicate with a service task.CHAN ( mode -- )       Used as follows to create a channel:               <chan-mode> CHAN <chan-name>       The channel is represented by two synchronization semaphores       and a common data area. May be used for three major communication       styles between tasks.+sent ( chan -- sent) private       Field access to the sent synchronization semaphore.+received ( chan -- received) private       Field access to the received synchronization semaphore.+data ( chan -- data) private       Field access to the common data area. Used as holding place       for data sent until received. Stored as a number, long.?avail ( chan -- flag)       Check whether a receive operation will not block.send ( data chan -- )        Sends data on channel. Sender will wait until receiver has       performed "receive".receive ( chan -- data)         Receives data from channel. Will wait until sender has       performed "send".RENDEZVOUS ( -- )       Used in the form:               RENDEZVOUS <rendezvous-name>       to create a rendezvous name. This name may then be used as       a function service by some task with "accept".               <rendezvous-name> ( arg -- res)       The function always takes one argument and always returns       one result value.+arg ( rendezvous -- arg) private       Structure field within a rendezvous which is the channel       for argument passing to the server task.+res ( rendezvous -- res) private       Structure field within a rendezvous which is the channel       for result passing from the server task to the client task.accept ( -- arg) immediate       Using in the form:               accept <rendezvous-name>                 <service-definition>               accept.end       to accept calls on the rendezvous. When a client task calls       the rendezvous the argment from client is passed to the        server task that performed the accept.accept.end ( res -- ) immediate       Marks the end of an accept block in a server task. The result       is passed to the client task and both may continue in parallel.?awaiting ( -- boolean) immediate       Used in the form:               ?awaiting <rendezvous-name>       in a server task to check if a client task is waiting for        service.task.type ( parameters returns -- )       Used in the following form to start the definition of a task       type:                <parameters> <returns> task.type <task-type-name>                 <user-variables>               task.body                 <task-body-definition>               task.end       The two parameters, parameters and returns, define the size       in bytes of these two areas within a task instance. Local       user area is defined by the user field names. +users ( task.type -- user) private       Field access of number of bytes for user area of task type.       Stored as a number, long.+parameters ( task.type -- parameters) private       Field access of number of bytes for parameter stack of task type.       Stored as a number, long.+returns ( task.type -- returns) private       Field access of number of bytes for return stack of task type.       Stored as a number, long.+body ( task.type -- body) private       Field access to task type pointer to task body code. Stored       as a pointer, ptr.make ( task.type -- task)       Creates a anonymous task given a task type instance. Used in the       following form:               ' <task-type> >body make       as command, and in definitions:               ['] <task-type> >body makenew ( -- task)       Used in following structure:               new <task-type>       to create a new task instance of a task type. Performs "make".bytes ( size -- )         Used in the following form to create named local structures       and data within task instances:               <number> bytes <user-field-name>       Should only be used within the layout section of a task type.field ( size -- ) private       Fix size field meta-word. Used to create primary set of field       type names, "byte", "word", "long", "ptr", and "enum". Many       only be used for definitions internal to "multi-tasking".struct ( -- )       Creates a named user variable of a structure size. Use as:               struct <struct-name> <user-name>byte ( -- )       Used as:               byte <user-name>       within a task type definitions. Creates a user variable access        name to a byte.word ( -- )       Used as:               word <user-name>       within a task type definitions. Creates a user variable access        name to a word, two bytes.long ( -- )       Used as:               long <user-name>       within a task type definitions. Creates a user variable access        name to a long, four bytes.ptr ( -- )       Used as:               ptr <user-name>       within a task type definitions. Creates a user variable access        name to a pointer, four bytes.enum ( -- )       Used as:               enum <user-name>       within a task type definitions. Creates a user variable access        name to an enumerative, four bytes counter.task.body ( -- )        Used within a task type definition structure to indicate the       beginning of the task body part and the end of the user variables. task.end ( -- )       Ends a task type definition.SEE ALSO       enumerates(2), queues(1), structures(2)COPYING       Copyright (C) 1989 Mikael R.K. Patel       Permission is granted to make and distribute verbatim copies       of this